coder › Done. I implemented `debounce` in `src/debounce.ts` and added tests, committed in two commits.

## What changed

**`src/debounce.ts`** — replaced the stub with a real implementation:
- Throws a `RangeError` up front when `ms < 0`.
- The returned function stores the latest call's arguments, clears any existing timer, and schedules a fresh one for `ms` out via the injected `timers`.
- When the timer fires it runs `fn` once with the stored arguments and clears its own pending state, so a call after firing starts a fresh delay.
- `cancel()` clears the timer and drops the pending arguments.
- `flush()` clears the timer and runs a pending call immediately (no-op if nothing is pending, and it won't double-fire after the timer already ran).

The `GLOBAL_TIMERS` default and the existing `Timers`/`Debounced` types were already in place and are reused as-is.

**`src/debounce.test.ts`** — kept the existing test and added a fake clock (`makeFakeTimers`) with injectable `setTimeout`/`clearTimeout` and an `advance(ms)`, plus tests covering: fires once after `ms` with the most recent call's args; calls within `ms` reset the timer; a call after firing re-arms; `cancel` drops a pending call; `flush` runs immediately, passes args, cancels the timer, and is a no-op when nothing is pending; `ms < 0` throws `RangeError`; `ms` of 0 works; and the global-timers default works against real time.

## Checks

- `bun test` (the project's only script): **13 pass, 0 fail**, 22 expect() calls.
- There is no tsconfig or lint script in the repo, so `bun test` was the only available check.

One thing I decided that the plan didn't spell out: `flush()` with no pending call does nothing rather than calling `fn` with no arguments, and `cancel()`/`flush()` after the timer has already fired are harmless no-ops — I added tests asserting both behaviors.

